    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `BasicErrorChecker._check_in_loop`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `_check_in_loop` function is a part of the `BasicErrorChecker` class in the `pylint/checkers/base/basic_error_checker.py` file. It checks whether a given node, which can be either a `Continue` or `Break` statement, is located inside a `for` or `while` loop. This function is crucial for ensuring that `continue` and `break` statements are used correctly within loops, as using them outside of loops would result in a syntax error.\n\nThe function takes two parameters: `node`, which is the node to be checked and can be either a `Continue` or `Break` statement, and `node_name`, which is a string representing the name of the node (either \"continue\" or \"break\"). The function does not return any value; instead, it adds messages to the checker's message queue if it detects an error.\n\nThe function relies on the `node_ancestors` method of the node to traverse up the abstract syntax tree (AST) and find the nearest parent nodes. It checks each parent node to see if it is a `For` or `While` loop. If the node is found within such a loop and not in the `orelse` block (the block executed if the loop condition is false), the function returns immediately, indicating that the node is correctly placed.\n\nIf the function traverses up the AST and reaches a `ClassDef` or `FunctionDef` node, it breaks out of the loop, as these nodes indicate that the node is not inside a loop. Additionally, if the node is a `Continue` statement and is found in the `finalbody` of a `Try` node (the block executed after all other blocks in a `try` statement), and the Python version is less than 3.8, the function adds a specific message about using `continue` in a `finally` block, which is not allowed in Python versions before 3.8.\n\nIf the function completes its traversal without finding a valid loop parent, it adds a general message indicating that the node is not inside a loop. This function does not handle any exceptions explicitly; instead, it relies on the robustness of the AST traversal methods it uses. The primary side effect of this function is the addition of messages to the checker's message queue, which can be used to report errors or warnings to the user.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
